{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfun3eJwbRfwH74DRTHkC","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Yes, mechanically. An Agent is a Durable Object, so `env.MyAgent.get(env.MyAgent.newUniqueId())` returns a normal DO stub, and you can call its public methods over JS RPC (compatibility date 2024-04-03 or later).\n\nThe catch is that the Agents SDK, which is built on partyserver, assumes every instance has a **name**. `getAgentByName()` / `idFromName()` set that name, but a `newUniqueId()` ID has none. Anything that reads `this.name` can therefore throw an error like \"Attempting to read .name … before it was set.\" That includes state sync, WebSocket/`routeAgentRequest` routing, scheduling, and some lifecycle hooks. Routing from clients like `useAgent` also won't work, because it addresses agents by name.\n\nIn practice, use `getAgentByName(env.MyAgent, someName)` instead. If you want a unique instance, pass a random string such as `crypto.randomUUID()` as the name. Use raw `newUniqueId()` only if your RPC methods avoid name-dependent Agent features. Check the docs for your SDK version, since newer releases have been relaxing some of these name requirements."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2263,"cache_read_input_tokens":535,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2263},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"27329538-76ef-46c1-a82c-1930102c8df7","uuid":"d83ee664-8265-4f78-b210-3c08969d8da6","timestamp":"2026-10-11T03:57:11.923Z","request_id":"req_011Cfun3e7Y4gDDdNF6HiSkf"}
